# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cyber Sentinel XDR — reverse proxy (Windows-native, via Caddy)
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#   uvicorn runs on asyncio's Windows ProactorEventLoop. An aggressive port scan
#   or malicious traffic hitting uvicorn's public port can kill its accept loop
#   (WinError 64 "network name is no longer available") — the backend then goes
#   "zombie": the process stays alive but stops accepting ANY new connections, so
#   every agent + the dashboard drop and the endpoint goes offline.
#
#   There is no reliable app-code fix for that OS-level asyncio fragility. The
#   permanent fix — used by every production Python web service — is to put a
#   hardened reverse proxy in front of the app. Caddy (a battle-tested Go server)
#   absorbs the raw/malicious connection traffic and forwards only clean HTTP to
#   uvicorn, which now binds loopback ONLY and never sees a scan again. This works
#   for EVERY user and endpoint, needs no code change, and runs natively on Windows.
#
# TOPOLOGY
#   endpoints / browsers ──► Caddy (0.0.0.0:8000) ──► uvicorn (127.0.0.1:8001)
#
#   Everything already targets port 8000, so NOTHING in the agent or frontend
#   changes: Caddy takes over :8000, and uvicorn simply moves to loopback :8001.
#
# INSTALL CADDY (Windows)
#   winget install CaddyServer.Caddy      (or download caddy.exe from caddyserver.com)
#
# RUN (two terminals)
#   1) Backend on loopback:
#        cd Backend
#        venv\Scripts\activate
#        uvicorn backend:sio_app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8001
#   2) Caddy from the repo root (this folder):
#        caddy run --config Caddyfile
#
#   The agent's XDR_BACKEND_URL and the dashboard both keep using :8000 — they now
#   reach Caddy, which forwards to the shielded backend.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# --- Plain HTTP reverse proxy (LAN / demo) --------------------------------------
# This alone fixes the scan / WinError-64 problem. No certificates required.
# A bare :port address makes Caddy serve plain HTTP (no auto-HTTPS), which is what
# we want on a LAN. Caddy v2 proxies WebSocket (Socket.IO) upgrades transparently,
# so no extra config is needed for the real-time event bus.
:8000 {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8001
}

# --- HTTPS add-on (recommended for real deployment) -----------------------------
# Your API key currently travels in plaintext HTTP. To encrypt everything, comment
# out the :8000 block above and use ONE of the blocks below instead. Caddy handles
# certificates automatically.
#
# (a) Public domain — Caddy auto-provisions + renews a real Let's Encrypt cert:
#
#   xdr.yourcompany.com {
#       reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8001
#   }
#
# (b) LAN / internal — Caddy's built-in CA issues a self-signed cert:
#
#   https://192.168.100.127 {
#       tls internal
#       reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8001
#   }
#
#   Then either run `caddy trust` (installs Caddy's root CA) on each Windows
#   endpoint so the agent trusts the cert, or point the agent at the https URL and
#   allow the internal CA. Agents/dashboard would then use https://<server> (443).
